content:
The buttons inside `DocumentToolbar` accept `ToolbarButtonProps`:

```ts
{
  document?: PHDocument;
  children?: ReactNode;
  className?: string;
  onClick?: (document?: PHDocument) => void;
}
```

`className` is merged into the button's defaults via `twMerge`, `children` replaces the default button contents, and `onClick` replaces the default behavior. Their default styling is:

- **Undo/Redo/Download/Switchboard/History/Close**: the shared `ToolbarButton` base — `grid size-fit place-items-center rounded-lg border border-border bg-background p-1 text-foreground` plus hover/active/disabled effects
- **Download button**: same base, with a `<span className="px-1 text-xs">Download</span>` label instead of an icon
- **Document name**: `<h1>` with `text-sm font-medium text-muted-foreground` (`ToolbarName` takes `titleClassName` / `inputClassName`)

Every button is exported individually (`ToolbarButton`, `ToolbarUndoButton`, `ToolbarRedoButton`, `ToolbarDownloadButton`, `ToolbarSwitchboardButton`, `ToolbarHistoryButton`, `ToolbarCloseButton`, `ToolbarName`), so the way to customize one is to wrap it and pass it back through `componentOverrides`:

```tsx
<DocumentToolbar
  document={document}
  componentOverrides={{
    download: (props) => (
      <ToolbarDownloadButton
        {...props}
        className="!bg-[#1e1e2e] !border-white/10"
      >
        <span>Export</span>
      </ToolbarDownloadButton>
    ),
  }}
/>
```

Or render the buttons yourself inside `children` for complete control over the toolbar contents.

Tailwind arbitrary child selectors (`[&_button]`, `[&_button_svg]`, `[&_h1]`, ...) passed through `toolbarClassName` still work as a blanket theming shortcut, but they are no longer required — they were a workaround for buttons that did not expose style props, and the buttons now do.

_Corrected 2026-08-13 against Powerhouse v6.2.2-dev.45._
